  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The lead was there to hold. Through five innings, perfectly balanced. In the sixth, it slipped away for good.",
    "what_this_means": "Luis Severino held Baltimore to two runs across 5.1 innings on six hits. That was enough. The offense scored once. In the eighth inning, with runners in scoring position, the Athletics left two on base. That moment. Two outs, a chance to tie or take the lead. Defined the game more than any scoreline. The bullpen was sharp. Justin Sterner and Luis Medina combined for 2.2 scoreless innings, struck out six. None of it mattered because the bats refused to arrive when the game was open. This is what inconsistency looks like: one quality outing from the starter, one clean bridge from the pen, and still a loss because the lineup couldn't execute in a single late window. The pattern from early season isn't broken. It's getting louder.",
    "what_to_watch": "St. Louis sends Andre Pallante to the mound tonight. His ERA sits at 4.34. The Cardinals' offense has struggled in recent weeks. If the Athletics starter can match Severino's line, the difference will be whether the lineup finally converts a chance in the middle innings.",
